    'ops/observation.md': '''
      **Otter Field Guide**
      
      - [Overview](../index.md)
      - [Species](../01-species.md)
      - [Habitat & range](../02-habitat.md)
      - **Field practice**
        - [Observation](./observation.md)
        - [Safety](./safety.md)
      
      # Observation
      
      *Field practice · A short protocol for spotting and recording otter activity without disturbing the animals.*
      
      > [!IMPORTANT] TL;DR
      > Stay downwind, downstream, and silent. Otters are wary of humans and will abandon a holt that feels exposed. Use a 10× monocular and start from a screened position at dawn or dusk.
      
      ## Protocol
      
      1. **Choose a screened vantage**
         Reedbed, hide, or a dense alder thicket — anything that breaks your silhouette and absorbs sound.
      2. **Wait silently**
         Allow at least 20 minutes for ambient wildlife to forget you are there.
      3. **Record bare-eye, sketch on paper**
         Phones glow. Sketch in pencil; transcribe later.
      
      ## What to record
      
      - [ ] Time, weather, water height
      - [ ] Activity (foraging, grooming, transit)
      - [ ] Direction of travel and last known position
      - [ ] Any vocalisations
      
  
    ''',
    'ops/safety.md': '''
      **Otter Field Guide**
      
      - [Overview](../index.md)
      - [Species](../01-species.md)
      - [Habitat & range](../02-habitat.md)
      - **Field practice**
        - [Observation](./observation.md)
        - [Safety](./safety.md)
      
      # Safety
      
      *Field practice · Brief notes on staying safe around wild otters and the waterbodies they inhabit.*
      
      > [!WARNING]
      > Otters bite, and giant otters in particular will defend a den aggressively. Never approach a holt. If an animal is on shore between you and the water, retreat — never block its escape route.
      
      ## Personal safety
      
      - [ ] Keep at least 15 metres of distance
      - [ ] Never feed or attempt to handle
      - [ ] Carry a whistle for emergency signalling
      - [ ] Watch the bank for current and slip hazards
      
      ## Animal welfare
      
      Disturbance is a measurable stressor. Limit each session to 90 minutes and rotate vantage points across visits so the same family group is not repeatedly observed.
      
  
    ''',
  })
