  **412** — Refresh failures (▲ +27%)
  
  **48.9K** — Active sessions (→ ±0.5%)
  
  **3,841** — Auth attempts blocked (▲ +62%)
  
  ## What shipped this week
  
  - [x] EdDSA key rotation runbook tested in staging (@dana, due 2026-05-08)
  - [x] Refresh-token reuse detection (RFC 6749 §10.4) (@kim, due 2026-05-09)
  - [x] Move /auth/login to the new rate limiter (@dana, due 2026-05-10)
  - [x] Add p99 latency dashboard (@kim, due 2026-05-12)
  
  ## In progress
  
  - [ ] Refresh-failure spike investigation (see callout below) (@dana, due 2026-05-15)
  - [ ] Webauthn support behind a feature flag (@kim, due 2026-05-17)
  - [ ] Migrate session store to Postgres 16 (@dana, due 2026-05-20)
  
  > [!WARNING] Refresh-failure spike
  > Refresh failures climbed from ~320/day to 412/day this week (+27%). Early signal: about 60% of failures share a small set of UA strings consistent with one mobile build that's caching tokens past expiry.
  >
  > **Plan:** reach out to the mobile lead, gather build numbers, and decide whether to ship a server-side mitigation or wait for the next mobile release.
  
  ## Risks
  
  ### Key-rotation downtime window
  
  Production rotation is scheduled for 2026-05-21 03:00 UTC. JWKS cache TTL is 5 min; we expect a ≤5min window where some clients still trust the old key. Mitigation: pre-warm the new key at JWKS for 24h ahead.
  
  ### Postgres 16 migration
  
  Cutover is dual-write for 48h then primary switch. Rollback path: flip a single DSN and replay the WAL.
  
  ## Decisions logged
  
  <details>
  <summary>Refresh-token rotation: opaque vs JWT</summary>
  
  We picked **opaque** refresh tokens stored server-side. Reasoning: refresh tokens get reused, replayed, and revoked far more than access tokens; the size and signature cost of JWT buys us nothing for that role.
  
  Access tokens remain JWT (EdDSA) so service-to-service paths stay stateless.
  
  </details>
  
  <details>
  <summary>Why we deferred OAuth device flow</summary>
  
  One first-party client requested device flow for a CLI tool. We deferred — the use case is satisfied by a long-lived API token with scope restrictions, which is simpler and already supported. Revisit in Q3 if a second client asks.
  
  </details>
  
  ## Next two weeks
  
  - **2026-05-15** — Refresh-failure mitigation decided — Either ship server-side fix or commit to mobile-only fix and accept the spike.
  - **2026-05-17** — Webauthn behind flag — Internal users only. Public flip targeted for the following sprint.
  - **2026-05-20** — Postgres 16 dual-write begins — 48h soak. Cutover the morning of 2026-05-22.
  - **2026-05-21 03:00 UTC** — Key rotation in production — JWKS pre-warmed since 2026-05-20.
  
  
