  # An editorial frame for AI-authored documents.
  
  *Component showcase · Every tag, every variant, in one document — the way a real richdoc should be read top-to-bottom. · v0.4 · Updated this build · Platform team*
  
  - **Audience:** Agents writing for humans
  - **Vocabulary:** ~70 tags · 40+ components
  - **Status:** `[stable]` `[expanded vocabulary]`
  
  > [!IMPORTANT] TL;DR
  > *Richdoc* is a small framework for documents written by an agent and read by a person in a browser. This page exercises every tag in the vocabulary the way a real document should — read top-to-bottom, with occasional asides dropping down to a Notes section at the foot of the page.
  
  > [!NOTE] Reader controls (bottom-right cog)
  > Every richdoc ships with a floating **preview picker** in the bottom-right corner. Open it to switch between the two themes (*Editorial* / *Graphite*), three modes (*Light* / *Dark* / *Auto*), and four page widths (*Narrow* / *Standard* / *Wide* / *Full*). Your choices persist per file. Suppress the picker with `<rd-page prefs="off">`.
  >
  > Squeeze the page to **Narrow** in a wide browser to see the multi-column layouts, stat tiles, and TOC adapt to the chosen content width — responsiveness is driven by container queries on `rd-page`, not the viewport.
  
  The look is intentionally editorial: warm paper, Fraunces display, Geist body, hairlines instead of cards, oldstyle figures, and the occasional pull-quote.
  
  ## Callouts
  
  Five callout types, each with a Lucide icon and tracked small-caps title. They are quieter than the old filled blocks — a tinted background and a 2 px left rule do the work.
  
  > [!NOTE] What this is
  > Callouts break out short asides — context, caveats, or pointers — that you do not want buried in a paragraph.
  
  > [!TIP] Recommended
  > Prefer callouts over bold-italic emphasis for anything more than a sentence.
  
  > [!WARNING] Heads up
  > `rd-cols` collapses to a single column under ~720 px. Do not put more than four columns or content becomes unreadable on tablets.
  
  > [!CAUTION] Don't
  > Never invent new `rd-*` tags. The linter fails and the rendered doc shows empty boxes.
  
  > [!NOTE]
  > Title is optional. Without one, the callout uses the type name as a fallback heading.
  
  ## Cards in columns
  
  Use `rd-cols` for genuinely parallel content. Cards are the natural child. Each accent value lights up a top rule and a coloured kicker label so the card reads typographically, not as a tinted block.
  
  ### Three cards, no accents
  
  ### Discovery
  
  Find out what exists. Read the code, run small probes, talk to whoever knows the system best.
  
  ### Design
  
  Decide what to change. Resolve the trade-offs explicitly. Write them down before touching anything.
  
  ### Delivery
  
  Execute the plan in reviewable units. Keep changes small and traceable. Ship boring code.
  
  ### Four cards with accent colours
  
  ### Info
  
  Neutral context, links, references.
  
  ### Success
  
  Positive outcomes, recommendations.
  
  ### Warn
  
  Things to watch out for, fragile areas.
  
  ### Danger
  
  Hard blockers, breaking changes.
  
  ### Asymmetric layout via `template`
  
  Set `template` on `rd-cols` for a CSS-Grid string that defines arbitrary column ratios. Below 720 px it still collapses to one column.
  
  ### Main column
  
  Use the wide column for the primary thought. Use the narrow column for a related card, a quote, or a small comparison.
  
  This pattern is editorial-native: think of it as a body column with a sidebar.
  
  Muted cards are for asides, footnotes, or quiet credits. No border; italic by default.
  
  ## Stats
  
  Use `rd-stat` for dashboard tiles. The numeral is set in Fraunces at optical size 144 with tabular figures, sitting under a hairline rule — confident, not boxy.
  
  **42.6K** — Requests / day (▲ +8.2%)
  
  **184 ms** — p95 latency (▼ -12 ms)
  
  **0.31%** — Error rate (▲ +0.04 pp)
  
  **3** — Open incidents (→ no change)
  
  ## Pull-quotes
  
  Pull-quotes use the native `<blockquote>` element — styled by `richdoc.css` with an oversized opening glyph, Fraunces italic, and small-caps attribution via `<cite>` or `<footer>`.
  
  > Instead of imagining that our main task is to instruct a computer what to do, let us concentrate rather on explaining to human beings what we want a computer to do.
  >
  >  Donald Knuth — Literate Programming, 1984
  
  ## Comparison tables
  
  For decision matrices. Tones get a coloured dot prefix and a low-opacity stripe — readable in print, distinctive on screen.
  
  | Aspect | Markdown | Pure HTML | richdoc |
  | --- | --- | --- | --- |
  | Layout primitives | ✗ No multi-column, no cards | · Possible, verbose | ✓ Built in |
  | Authoring vocabulary | ✓ Tiny, universal | ✗ Unbounded — drifts | ✓ Closed, linted |
  | LLM authoring reliability | · Good | ✗ Drifts under load | ✓ Validated at write time |
  | Render anywhere | ✓ Yes — GitHub renders it | ✓ Yes — any browser | · Browser only |
  
  ## Key/value metadata
  
  Use `rd-kv` for short status blocks at the top of a doc. No outer box, just hairlines.
  
  - **Owner:** platform team
  - **Status:** `[in progress]`
  - **Priority:** `[P0]`
  - **Tags:** `[infra]` `[auth]` `[security]`
  - **Notes:** Values can contain inline HTML, badges, links — anything inline.
  
  ## Code blocks
  
  Code lives in `rd-code`. The bar at the top shows the language as a tracked small-caps kicker and an optional title in Fraunces italic. Syntax highlighting loads on demand from highlight.js and is themed against the editorial palette — no third-party stylesheet.
  
  ```typescript
  // auth.ts
  import { signToken } from "./jwt.ts";
  import type { TokenPayload } from "./types.ts";
  
  export async function authenticate(payload: TokenPayload): Promise<string> {
  	const key = await loadSigningKey();
  	if (!key) {
  		throw new Error("signing key unavailable");
  	}
  	return signToken(payload, key, { algorithm: "EdDSA" });
  }
  ```
  
  ```bash
  # Initialize a richdoc next to your file
  cd docs/
  richdoc init .
  richdoc new plan.html --template plan
  ```
  
  ## Diffs
  
  `rd-diff` renders a unified diff with additions and deletions colour-coded. If `lang` is set, the line bodies are syntax-highlighted by the same engine that powers `rd-code`.
  
  ```diff
  // auth.ts
  @@ -3,5 +3,10 @@
   import { signToken } from "./jwt.ts";
   import type { TokenPayload } from "./types.ts";
  
  -export async function authenticate(payload: TokenPayload): Promise<string> {
  -	const key = await loadSigningKey();
  -	return signToken(payload, key, { algorithm: "EdDSA" });
  +export async function authenticate(
  +	payload: TokenPayload,
  +	opts: AuthOptions = {},
  +): Promise<Token> {
  +	const key = await loadSigningKey(opts.keyId);
  +	if (!key) throw new AuthError("signing key unavailable");
  +	return signToken(payload, key, { algorithm: opts.algorithm ?? "EdDSA" });
   }
  ```
  
  ## Math
  
  Use `rd-math` for KaTeX-rendered formulas. Block display by default; pass `display="inline"` for inline use, e.g. the relation $E = mc^2$ can sit inside a sentence. Block displays render between hairline rules:
  
  $$
  P(A \mid B) = \frac{P(B \mid A)\, P(A)}{P(B)}
  $$
  
  $$
  \frac{\partial}{\partial t}\Psi(\mathbf{r}, t) = -\frac{i}{\hbar} \hat{H}\, \Psi(\mathbf{r}, t)
  $$
  
  ## Icons
  
  Inline SVG glyphs from the full Lucide library (~1,900 names at the pinned `lucide-static` version). A core set ships inline in `richdoc.js`; everything else is lazy-loaded from jsDelivr on first reference and cached. Run `richdoc components --tag rd-icon` for the authoritative list.
  
  ### Status
  
  check  x  warn  info
  
  ### Direction
  
  up  down  next  out
  
  ### Editorial
  
  sparkles  zap  star  bookmark
  
  ### Lazy (CDN)
  
  heart  compass  cloud  database  cpu  package  rocket  telescope
  
  Sizes:  small,  medium,  large.
  
  ## Tabs
  
  Tabbed content for the same idea expressed three ways. First tab is active; arrow keys navigate.
  
  ### Overview
  
  Tabs are good for the same content rendered three ways: language variants, before/after, examples by stack.
  
  They are **not** for hiding important decisions — humans scanning the doc will miss whatever isn't on the active tab.
  
  ### When to use
  
  - Same example in multiple languages.
  - Before / after code comparisons.
  - Optional deep dives that interrupt the main flow.
  
  ### When NOT to use
  
  - Hiding important steps. Use sections instead.
  - Long content. Tabs cap at a screenful of comfortable reading.
  - Search-critical content (only the active tab is visible to Ctrl+F on first load).
  
  ## Timeline
  
  Sequenced events: release history, project milestones, incident postmortems. Dates in tracked small-caps, titles in Fraunces italic, the rule is dotted.
  
  - **2026-05-14** — v0.1 PoC — Initial vocabulary (17 tags), three templates, smoke-tested CLI.
  - **2026-05-15** — v0.2 framework refactor — Modular source tree, build pipeline, schema co-located with each component, five new tags.
  - **2026-05-16** — v0.3 editorial redesign — Fraunces + Geist + Fira Code, hairline rules, pull-quotes, oversized stats. Five new tags (`rd-icon`, `rd-math`, `rd-diff`, glossary support via `rd-kv layout="stacked"`); `rd-code` upgraded with syntax highlighting and line numbers.
  - **TBD** — v1.0 multi-doc sites — `richdoc site` for static collections with sidebar nav and search.
  
  ## Diagrams
  
  One element, ~25 diagram languages. Source is sent to a Kroki-compatible endpoint (default `kroki.io`) and the rendered SVG embeds inline. Set `endpoint` per-element or `<rd-page diagram-endpoint="…">` for the whole doc; for sensitive content point at a self-hosted Kroki. Hover any diagram and click the corner **maximize** button for the fullscreen pan/zoom viewer (0 fit, 1 reset, arrow keys pan, Esc close).
  
  ```mermaid
  flowchart LR
  	A[Agent writes .html] --> B{richdoc lint}
  	B -- OK --> C[Open in browser]
  	B -- errors --> D[Fix and re-run]
  	D --> B
  	C --> E[Human review]
  ```
  
  ```plantuml
  @startuml
  actor Agent
  participant "richdoc CLI" as CLI
  database Browser
  Agent -> CLI : richdoc lint doc.html
  CLI --> Agent : ok
  Agent -> Browser : open doc.html
  Browser --> Agent : rendered page
  @enduml
  ```
  
  ```d2
  Browser -> API: POST /login
  API -> DB: verify
  API -> Browser: set cookie
  ```
  
  ## Figures
  
  Wrap an image, SVG, or diagram with a centred caption. The caption is set in Fraunces italic with a "Fig." kicker in the accent colour.
  
  ```mermaid
  flowchart TB
  	Plan --> Draft --> Validate --> Publish
  	Validate -- fail --> Draft
  ```
  
  *Document lifecycle: author, validate, render, review.*
  
  ## Collapsible details
  
  Use `rd-detail` for content that is relevant but not on the main path — appendices, full error logs, derivations. Native `<details>` under the hood, so it works without JS and remains keyboard-accessible.
  
  <details>
  <summary>Why a fixed vocabulary instead of free-form HTML?</summary>
  
  Free-form HTML lets agents drift. One doc invents `<callout>`, the next uses `<note>`, the third leaves it as bold-italic prose. The reader experience fragments and the agent has no way to verify their output.
  
  A closed vocabulary plus a validator makes both ends of the loop reliable: the agent always knows what is allowed, and the reader always sees the same components.
  
  </details>
  
  <details open>
  <summary>Open by default</summary>
  
  Pass the `open` attribute to start expanded.
  
  </details>
  
  ## Checklists
  
  For action items with optional assignee and due date. Hairline-separated rows, ticks in display face when complete.
  
  - [x] Land v0.1 PoC (@dana, due 2026-05-10)
  - [x] Split sources into per-component folders (@dana, due 2026-05-13)
  - [x] Add build pipeline + schema.json (@dana, due 2026-05-14)
  - [x] Editorial redesign + vocabulary expansion (@dana, due 2026-05-16)
  - [ ] Static-site mode for multi-doc collections (@kim, due 2026-05-25)
  
  ## Badges
  
  Inline status pills, prefixed with a coloured dot: `[default]` `[info]` `[success]` `[warn]` `[danger]` `[muted]`
  
  ## Glossary
  
  Use `rd-kv layout="stacked"` for a definition list. Terms are set in Fraunces italic, the block is bracketed by hairline rules, rows separated by dotted lines.
  
  **Terms used in this document**
  
  *richdoc*
  : A small framework for AI-authored, human-read HTML documents that uses a closed vocabulary of `rd-*` custom elements.
  
  *editorial*
  : A design voice borrowed from print publications: hairline rules, oldstyle figures, drop caps, Fraunces display type, restrained colour.
  
  *closed vocabulary*
  : A fixed list of tags that the agent must choose from. New tags require a deliberate authoring decision and a linter update.
  
  *optical size*
  : A Fraunces variation axis that adjusts letterforms for the size at which they will appear. Set higher (`72`, `144`) for display.
  
  ## TL;DR strip
  
  Use `<rd-callout type="tldr">` as the first block after the hero. It's a focal summary band with an oversized eyebrow and slightly larger body type than the surrounding prose. See the very top of this page.
  
  ## Hero
  
  Use `rd-hero` instead of an ad-hoc `<h1>` + `rd-kv` opener. It coordinates eyebrow, title, lede, and meta into one display block, and accepts any inline children (e.g. an `rd-kv`) as an "extras" strip below the meta line. The top of this page demonstrates the full structure.
  
  ## Banner
  
  Doc-status ribbons sit at the top of the page. Five types:
  
  > [!NOTE]
  > **DRAFT** — draft
  
  > [!NOTE]
  > **FROZEN** — frozen
  
  > [!NOTE]
  > **ARCHIVED** — archived
  
  > [!NOTE]
  > **CONFIDENTIAL** — confidential
  
  > [!NOTE]
  > **INFO** — Custom message overrides the default text for the type.
  
  ## Progress
  
  Linear progress / capacity bar. Accepts a 0..1 decimal, `N%`, or `N/M` fraction. The fill animates from 0 to the target on entry.
  
  **Migration complete:** 78%
  
  **Tests passing:** 12 / 20
  
  **Disk used:** 35%
  
  **Project budget consumed:** 45%
  
  ## Steps
  
  Numbered procedural steps with a rich body per step. Use this for runbooks, onboarding flows, migration guides. Distinct from `rd-timeline` (dated history) and `rd-checklist` (task tracking).
  
  1. **~~Drop the assets next to your doc~~**
     Run `richdoc init docs` once in the directory that will hold your `.html` file.
  2. **~~Scaffold a new document~~**
     Pick a template: `richdoc new docs/plan.html -t plan`.
  3. **Write the document**
     Use only the `rd-*` tags from the vocabulary. Plain HTML is welcome inside any block.
  4. **Validate before sharing**
     Run `richdoc lint docs/plan.html`. Linting cleanly is part of "done".
  
  ## Pros and cons
  
  Two-column ✓/✗ grid for decision documents. Distinct from `rd-compare` — that's a matrix; this is one-sided evaluation of a single option.
  
  #### Reasons to adopt
  - Already-shipped pattern in the existing vocabulary.
  - No new external dependencies in Phase 1.
  - Linter enforces shape on existing rule engine.
  
  #### Reasons to wait
  - Vocabulary grows from ~30 → ~70 entries.
  - Authors must learn the new tags.
  
  ## Decision records
  
  ADR-style decision header + rationale. Four statuses control the left rule colour and the status pill.
  
  ### ADR-014: Adopt Observable Plot for charting
  
  *`[ACCEPTED]` · 2026-05-14 · platform team*
  
  Bundle size is not a concern for documentation artefacts. Plot's grammar-of-graphics defaults match the editorial voice better than anything we'd hand-roll, and one library covers every chart type plus sparklines.
  
  ### ADR-015: Tufte-style margin notes as a separate component
  
  *`[REJECTED]` · 2026-05-14*
  
  A floating-gutter sidenote (`rd-margin`) was prototyped but rejected: the page column is centred at 1280px with no reserved gutter, so the negative-margin float overflowed off-screen on 1024–1500px viewports — the exact range the wide-mode media query targeted. `rd-cite` with `rd-ref` already covers the related authoring case on every viewport.
  
  ### ADR-007: Original hand-rolled SVG chart approach
  
  *`[SUPERSEDED]` · 2026-02-01*
  
  Superseded by ADR-014.
  
  ### ADR-008: Embedding D3 directly for every chart
  
  *`[REJECTED]` · 2026-02-15*
  
  Too much per-chart code; Plot covers the same surface with a much smaller author API.
  
  ## Status updates
  
  Dated reverse-chron entries for changelogs, release notes, status reports. The kind glyph and tint differ between release / change / note.
  
  ### 2026-05-14 — v0.4 — expanded vocabulary
  
  *release · platform team*
  
  Expanded the framework's structural vocabulary — hero, banner, progress, steps, pros/cons, decision records, updates, terminal sessions, API blocks, and rubrics all landed in one release.
  
  ### 2026-05-12 — Schema groups reorganised
  
  *change · dana*
  
  Introduced *Decision & planning* and *Reference* groupings in the schema registry. Mirrored in the SKILL.md tag tables.
  
  ### 2026-05-09 — Discovery doc accepted
  
  *note*
  
  The 20-component plan was reviewed and accepted. Implementation phases queued.
  
  ## FAQ
  
  Question/answer disclosure uses `rd-detail variant="question"` — questions render in display Fraunces, answers in body Inter, with hairline separators between entries.
  
  <details open>
  <summary>Do I have to use richdoc instead of Markdown?</summary>
  
  Use Markdown where the renderer might be anything (GitHub, chat, CLI). For documents read in a browser, richdoc gives a much richer presentation with no extra effort.
  
  </details>
  
  <details>
  <summary>Can I add my own tags?</summary>
  
  No — the linter will reject unknown `rd-*` tags. The fixed vocabulary is the point. Extensions ship through a new release.
  
  </details>
  
  <details>
  <summary>Does it work offline?</summary>
  
  Yes. The two shipped assets (`richdoc.css`, `richdoc.js`) work over `file://`. Components that need a CDN library (mermaid, KaTeX, charts) degrade to a readable fallback when offline.
  
  </details>
  
  ## Shell sessions
  
  Terminal transcripts for runbooks and tutorials. Distinct from `rd-code` — no syntax highlighting, no copy button, just prompt → output.
  
  ```bash
  // ~/Projects/agent-skills
  $ richdoc init docs
  copied richdoc.css → docs/richdoc.css
  copied richdoc.js  → docs/richdoc.js
  $ richdoc new docs/plan.html -t plan
  wrote docs/plan.html (1245 bytes)
  $ richdoc lint docs/plan.html
  {"ok": true, "issues": []}
  ```
  
  ## API reference
  
  Single-endpoint reference block. Method pill is coloured per HTTP verb; status pills tint themselves based on the 2xx/4xx/5xx class.
  
  ### `POST` `/v1/documents/{id}/validate` — Lint a single richdoc against the schema
  
  *auth:* `Bearer token`
  
  | Param | In | Required | Type | Default | Description |
  | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
  | `id` | path | ✓ | uuid |  | Document identifier. |
  | `strict` | query |  | boolean | false | Treat warnings as errors. |
  | `X-Trace-Id` | header |  | string |  | Optional client-supplied trace identifier. |
  | `body` | body | ✓ | text/html |  | The document to lint. |
  
  | Status | Type | Description |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | `200` | application/json | Lint succeeded. Body is `{ ok, issues[] }`. |
  | `400` | application/json | Malformed input. |
  | `422` | application/json | Schema validation failed; `issues[]` contains details. |
  
  ## Rubric (weighted scoring)
  
  Weighted scoring grid for comparison documents — like `rd-compare` but with numeric scores, weights, and an automatic totals row. The winning column highlights.
  
  ### Chart engine evaluation
  
  |   | Observable Plot | ApexCharts | Hand-rolled SVG |
  | --- | --- | --- | --- |
  | Editorial fit (×3) | 5 — FT/Economist defaults | 3 — SaaS dashboard | 5 — total control |
  | Chart types (×2) | 5 | 5 | 2 — bar/line/area/donut |
  | Author API (×2) | 4 | 5 | 3 |
  | Offline fallback (×1) | 4 | 4 | 5 — no CDN |
  | **Total** | **37** | **33** | **30** |
  
  ## Citations and references
  
  Bibliography support follows the footnote pattern but with square-bracketed numbers and a separate collector. Authors write `rd-cite` markers inline[1] and `rd-ref` entries anywhere in the doc[2]. The system numbers citations in document order, builds the bibliography below, and shows the entry as a tooltip when hovering a marker.
  
  Multiple cites to the same key share a number[1]. Entries not cited still appear in the bibliography, after the cited ones[3].
  
  ## Charts
  
  SVG charts via Observable Plot, lazy-loaded from jsDelivr on first use. Six kinds out of the box. If Plot can't load (offline, blocked CDN), each chart falls back to a clean table of the underlying data.
  
